  <content>
  <section><title>Deriving the Quadratic Formula Using an Example</title>
<para>Here is another example that goes through the process of deriving the <term data-schema-version="“1.0”" data-store="glossary-tooltip">quadratic formula</term>:</para>
<para>Original equation</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Multiply each side by 4. This helps us avoid some tricky fractions. (In general, multiply each side of the equation by 4 times the coefficient of the \(x^2\) term. In this case, that is just 4 times 1, which is 4.)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Subtract 16 from each side.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Rewrite.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Use \(P\) as a placeholder for \(2x\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Add to complete the square.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Write the left side as a squared factor.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 7</emphasis> - Find the square root of the expression on the right.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 8</emphasis> - Subtract 7 from both sides to isolate.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 9</emphasis> - Rearrange the expression under the square root sign.</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 10</emphasis> - </para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 11</emphasis> - </para>
<para>Notice the original equation, \(y=x^2+7x+4\), has \(a=1\), \(b=7\), \(c=4\), and the last step is of the form \(x=\frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a}\), which is the quadratic formula.</para>
<title>Try It: Deriving the Quadratic Formula Using an Example</title>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Jaiden was deriving the quadratic formula from the quadratic \(x^2+11x+5=0\). What is the step after the step shown below?</para>
    <para>\((x+\frac{11}{2})^2=\frac{11^2-4(1)(5)}{4(1)}\)</para>
    <para>\((x+\frac{11}{2})^2=\frac{121-20}{4}\)</para>
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Here is how to find the next step:</para>
  <para>To isolate \(x\), take the square root of both sides:</para>
  <para>\(\sqrt{(x+\frac{11}{2})^2}=\sqrt{\frac{121-20}{4}}\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
</content>
